C = [
("h1", "16. Objections and Replies"),
("p", "This chapter takes the strongest objections to the position and answers each from Scripture. The objections are stated as their holders state them. Two modern writers are named whose published words the author has read in full on a free text, Matt Perman, writing for Desiring God, and Thomas L. Constable, in his notes on Romans and on 1 Corinthians. They are quoted only from those texts. Perman draws on five sermons of John Piper on Romans 5:12–21 and quotes him in the article; Piper’s sermons themselves were not read for this volume, so Piper is quoted only as Perman quotes him, and no view is credited to him beyond that."),
("p", "Both writers fortify the thesis at some points and hold the opposite view at others. The chapter says which is which. Where an objection is held by a tradition whose individual writers were not read, the tradition is described in the volume’s words and no sentence is placed in any person’s mouth."),
("sub", "16.1 Objection: Romans 5 teaches that Adam’s sin is counted as every person’s sin"),
("p", "This is the reading of the federal or imputation tradition, and it is stated plainly by Matt Perman: “when Adam first sinned, that sin (and its blame) was rightly regarded by God to be our sin as well.” He supports it from the text in five steps, and he quotes John Piper for the force of it: “whose sin became our sin and whose judgment became our judgment.” His fourth step is the emphasis on the one transgression: “We are all condemned not ultimately because of our individual sins, but because of one sin (verse 18).” His fifth step is verse 19. Because the many are “made sinners” in a sentence paired with the many being “made righteous,” he argues, “it must also be referring to imputation.”"),
("p", "Thomas Constable holds the same reading. On Romans 5:12–14 he writes, “Verses 12 through 14 describe imputed sin, not inherited sin.” And in the same notes: “Adam’s sin was imputed directly to us because we are members of his race.” He adds a second ground, the natural headship of the father over the child, so that he holds two ways of transmission together, the inherited and the imputed."),
("p", "The volume answers in four steps. First, it agrees with what these writers see in the text and builds on it. Paul does say that the results come from one act. The word “one” stands twelve times between verse 12 and verse 19, and Perman is right that the emphasis falls on the one transgression. The volume reads that as the shape of the figure (chapter 4). It does not weaken it."),
("p", "Second, it asks what the text says of the connection between the one act and each person, and the text says less than the doctrine does. Verse 12 gives “for that all have sinned” as the ground on which death reached all men. Perman and Constable read the clause as meaning that all sinned in Adam. The verse does not contain the words //in Adam//. It says that all have sinned. The volume does not decide in the clause what the clause does not decide, and it does not claim that the imputation reading is excluded. It claims that the sentence does not state it."),
("p", "Third, the argument from verse 19 proves less than it is asked to prove. Perman is right that “made sinners” and “made righteous” are in one sentence and that the second is a standing, not an inward change. But the sentence is in a passage in which Paul has said three times that the gift is “not as” the offence (Romans 5:15, 16) and that the second half carries a condition the first does not: “they which receive” (Romans 5:17). If the parallel in verse 19 is exact, the many who are made righteous are all the many who were made sinners, and Paul’s own clause of reception has no work to do. The text does not allow the parallel to be pressed so far. It says what it says: the many were made sinners, and the many shall be made righteous, in sentences Paul has already warned are not equal."),
("p", "Fourth, the volume notes that the most that can be drawn from the sentence is that the one man’s act has results for the many. That is Paul’s claim, and it is the whole of the claim the thesis needs. Whether the results are best described by imputation, by inheritance or by both is a question the volume leaves where Paul leaves it."),
("sub", "16.2 Objection: “all” means that every person is saved"),
("p", "This reading takes “the free gift came upon all men unto justification of life” (Romans 5:18) to say that all men are justified, and it takes “in Christ shall all be made alive” (1 Corinthians 15:22) to say that all shall be saved. It is held by some writers who are called universalists. No such writer is named here, because none was read for this volume, and the volume does not credit this view to any person. It states the objection as its holders would state it: the “all” is the same in both halves, and the same as the “all men” of the first half, so that if all die in Adam, all must be made alive in Christ."),
("p", "The volume replies from the sentences themselves. In Romans 5 the clause of verse 17 stands in the same parenthesis as verse 18, and it says that those who reign in life are “they which receive.” Paul has also shown in the same letter that he can say “all” and bound it, as in “unto all and upon all them that believe” (Romans 3:22). In 1 Corinthians 15:22 the “all” of the second half is “all” in Christ, and the chapter has used “in Christ” of those who have fallen asleep in him and who hope in him (1 Corinthians 15:18, 19). The sentence does not say that every person is in Christ."),
("p", "Constable supports this point from the other side. On 1 Corinthians 15:21–22 he writes, “Obviously Paul was referring only to believers as descendants of Christ.” The word “obviously” is his, and the volume reaches the same conclusion by the route of the clause “in Christ.” He describes the passage in Romans as presenting “Adam and Christ as heads of two groups of people: all human beings, and believers, respectively.” That is the thesis in his words: two headships, with different membership."),
("sub", "16.3 Objection: 1 Corinthians 15:23 describes the order of all the resurrections"),
("p", "Constable reads the verse as the beginning of a larger list. “Christians are in a different rank and will experience resurrection together as a group and at a different time, namely, at the Lord’s coming.” That is the narrow reading of the verse in its own words: the believers form a rank of their own. He then adds, “There will be other ranks of people who will arise at other times including Tribulation saints, Old Testament believers, and the unsaved.” And he says plainly that the verse does not give the full account: “The apostle did not go on to give a complete explanation of the various resurrections here.”"),
("p", "The volume agrees with the first and third of these sentences, and it takes them as support. The verse names two ranks, Christ the firstfruits and those who are Christ’s. It does not name the others. Constable gets the others from other passages, and the volume does not dispute that other passages speak of other resurrections. They are the subject of Study 49, The Resurrections of Scripture, and of the Prophecy & Mystery Commentary Series, Volume 4, The Resurrection Order in Scripture, where the order and company of each are read from their own passages. This volume asks only that verse 23 not be made to carry them."),
("sub", "16.4 Objection: the last Adam makes the church the new Israel"),
("p", "This reading is held by a tradition in which the church is taken to be the true Israel and the heir of Israel’s promises, and in which Christ as the last Adam is the head of a renewed humanity of which Israel is a first stage. No writer is named here, because none was read for this volume, and the objection is stated in the volume’s words. It is a large claim, and the two passages are asked to carry it."),
("p", "The volume replies that the two passages do not name Israel at all. Romans 5:12–21 names Adam, Moses once as a point in time, the law and Jesus Christ, and speaks of “all men” and “many.” First Corinthians 15:21–22 and 45–49 name Adam and Christ, “the earthy” and “the heavenly.” Neither passage uses the name Israel, or the name of a covenant. A reader who finds a replaced Israel in them has supplied it from elsewhere. The volume does not say that the question is closed. It says that these two passages do not decide it, and that the weight of an argument about Israel cannot be laid on them."),
("sub", "16.5 Objection: the two passages teach one headship said twice"),
("p", "The last objection is the simplest. Paul says in Romans what he says in 1 Corinthians, and the two passages are best read as one teaching, so that any difference between them is a difference of wording. The objection has force, because the two passages share names, an enemy and a final title (chapter 14). Constable himself reads both as speaking of the two groups, all human beings and believers."),
("p", "The volume replies that the differences are not wording. The questions are different, the vocabulary is different, the joining word is different, the things said of the two men are different, and the verses that follow each passage take up different matters. A single headship said twice would not require the first to speak of an offence and a gift and the second of a living soul and a quickening spirit. Where the passages agree, the volume agrees with the objection, and it holds that they agree at the points where one writer would be expected to agree with himself."),
("ctbl", "The objections at a glance.", [
    ("Objection", "Who holds it, as read", "The reply in one line", "Where answered"),
    ("Romans 5 teaches that Adam’s sin is counted as every person’s sin", "Matt Perman (for Desiring God, quoting John Piper); Thomas Constable", "The one act is Paul’s emphasis, but verse 12 does not say //in Adam//, and the parallel of verse 19 is qualified by the “not as” clauses and by verse 17", "16.1; chapters 3, 4, 5"),
    ("“All” means every person is saved", "A universalist reading; no writer read, none named", "Verse 17 is in the same parenthesis; 1 Corinthians 15:22 is bounded by “in Christ”; Constable reads the verse as referring only to believers", "16.2; chapters 6, 9"),
    ("1 Corinthians 15:23 gives the order of all the resurrections", "Thomas Constable adds ranks from other passages", "The verse names two ranks; the others belong to Study 49 and to Volume 4 of the Prophecy & Mystery Commentary Series", "16.3; chapter 9"),
    ("The last Adam makes the church the new Israel", "A tradition; no writer read, none named", "Neither passage names Israel or a covenant", "16.4; chapter 14"),
    ("The two passages teach one headship said twice", "Constable reads the same two groups in both", "The questions, vocabulary, joining words and things said of the men differ", "16.5; chapters 1, 14"),
  ], [0.26, 0.22, 0.34, 0.18]),
]
